---
import CasePage from '../../components/case/CasePage.astro';
import Section from '../../components/case/Section.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/case/Plate.astro';
import Limits from '../../components/case/Limits.astro';
import SweetySchema from '../../components/case/figures/SweetySchema.astro';
---

<CasePage
  num="04"
  domain="Clinical software"
  title="Sweety"
  subtitle="A glucose tracker with a clinician on the other side."
  abstract="A patient records up to four readings a day. A doctor reads the same numbers in a different frame. Sweety is a small clinical data system built around that split — with every rule enforced in the service and again in the database."
  description="Sweety: a blood glucose tracker with four daily slots, three report ranges, and a doctor role, built on Express, PostgreSQL, Redis and React."
  year="2026"
  status="Repository · Docker stack"
  stack="Node · PostgreSQL · Redis · React"
  spec={[
    { label: 'Problem', value: 'A home glucose log is only useful if the numbers can be trusted and read.' },
    { label: 'Constraint', value: 'Four fixed slots, one reading per slot per day, 40–600 mg/dl, two roles.' },
    { label: 'Approach', value: 'Express and PostgreSQL, constraints plus a trigger, cached reports, a reminder job.' },
    { label: 'Result', value: '61 server tests and 6 client tests, a Docker Compose stack, and a doctor workspace.' },
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/sweety_Blood-G' },
    { label: 'API reference', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/sweety_Blood-G/blob/main/docs/DOCUMENTATION.md' },
    { label: 'Schema', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/sweety_Blood-G/blob/main/server/src/db/schema.sql' },
  ]}
  contents={[
    { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
    { id: 'method', label: 'The method' },
    { id: 'result', label: 'The result' },
    { id: 'limits', label: 'Known limits' },
  ]}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Repository', note: 'Client, API, worker, schema, seed.' },
    { label: 'Tests', note: '61 server, 6 client, real Postgres.' },
    { label: 'Screenshots', note: 'Ten states in docs/screenshots.' },
  ]}
  next={{ num: '01', title: 'VERDICT', href: '/work/verdict' }}
>
  <Section id="question" num="01" label="The question" title="A record kept by the least reliable witness.">
    <p>
      A glucose log is kept by the person with the least incentive to record the bad days. The design
      problem is not the chart. It is making the four daily readings cheap enough to enter, strict
      enough to trust, and readable by someone who was not there.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second user is the doctor. They need the same readings without the patient's navigation,
      and they should only see patients assigned to them.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="method" num="02" label="The method" title="Four slots, and a rule that holds twice.">
    <p>
      Four slots: morning fasting, after breakfast, after lunch, after dinner. One reading per slot
      per day, between 40 and 600 mg/dl, and no future dates. A day with three readings is a complete
      day with a gap, not an error.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rules are enforced twice. The service returns the assignment's message, and PostgreSQL holds
      a unique constraint, a check constraint and a trigger that refuses a fifth reading. If two
      requests race, the database wins.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reports are daily, month-to-date, and the previous calendar month. The average is rounded once,
      in SQL. Redis caches report responses for 300 seconds and every write invalidates them; if
      Redis is unavailable the API reads through to PostgreSQL. A BullMQ job scans at 08:00 for
      patients with fewer than four readings and writes a reminder log.
    </p>
    <p>
      Access is scoped at the query level, not in the client. Doctor assignment is by email, and the
      role in the token is not trusted — session restore calls <span class="mono-note">/auth/me</span>.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="figure">
      <Plate
        num="04"
        title="Schema and enforcement"
        caption="Four tables, one derived report surface. The constraints are not validation theatre: they make an invalid reading impossible if a request races another."
      >
        <SweetySchema />
      </Plate>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section id="result" num="03" label="The result" title="Tested against a real database.">
    <p>
      Sixty-one server tests and six client tests run against a real PostgreSQL database, not mocks.
      They cover password hashing, duplicate email, login failure, reading CRUD, ownership isolation,
      every validation message, the four-per-day limit, pagination, all three report ranges, doctor
      access, and reminder idempotency.
    </p>
    <p>
      The whole stack runs with <span class="mono-note">docker compose up</span>: client, API, worker,
      PostgreSQL 16 and Redis 7. The seed gives two patients roughly 50 and 24 days of readings and
      assigns both to one doctor, so an empty state is a choice rather than the default.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="limits" num="04" label="Known limits">
    <Limits>
      <p>
        The JWT is stored in <span class="mono-note">localStorage</span>. That is simple and testable,
        and readable by injected JavaScript; a production build would use httpOnly cookies, refresh
        rotation, CSRF protection and rate limiting on login.
      </p>
      <p>
        "Today" is the server's UTC date. A patient in India entering a late-night reading can land
        on the wrong calendar day; per-user timezones are the fix, and they are not implemented.
      </p>
      <p>
        Doctor assignment is by email with no invitation flow or admin role. The monthly report is the
        previous calendar month, not the last 30 days. The UI reads one day at a time even though the
        API paginates all readings.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no live demo in this build. The repository runs locally with Docker.
      </p>
    </Limits>
  </Section>
</CasePage>
